  "Question": "If the value of the algebraic fraction \\((x^2-9)/(x+3)\\) is 0, what is the value of \\(x\\)?",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "3",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "For the value to be 0, the numerator must be 0 and the denominator must be nonzero. Solving \\(x^2-9=0\\) gives \\(x=3\\) or \\(x=-3\\). However, when \\(x=-3\\), the denominator \\(x+3=0\\), so this value must be discarded. Therefore, \\(x=3\\).\n\nAlternative method 1: Factor and cancel on the original domain\n\nThe denominator requires \\(x\\ne-3\\). Factoring the numerator exposes the same factor as the denominator, so on this domain,\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2-9}{x+3}=\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)}{x+3}=x-3.\n\\]\nThe fraction is therefore 0 exactly when \\(x-3=0\\), giving \\(x=3\\), which satisfies the denominator restriction.",
